  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 133 AM CDT Fri Oct 16 2026\n\nA strong cold front can be seen via satellite and radar moving south \nto southeast across the Hill Country. The cold front is expected to \nmove through S TX later this morning, finally bringing some relief \nfrom the heat. The cooler and drier conditions will be more \nnoticeable tonight. A pleasant weekend is in store for S TX with \nhighs in the 70s and lows in the 50s to low 60s. Temperatures begin \nto warm back up early next week.\n\nRain chances associated with the cold front will be highest across \nthe Rio Grande Plains with up to 55% chance and across the gulf \nwaters with up to 70% across the offshore waters. The Brush Country, \nCoastal Plains and Victoria Crossroads have a 15-30% chance of \nmainly showers. A few thunderstorms will be possible. The rain is \nexpected to end from northwest to southeast this afternoon. \n\nStrong north winds behind the cold front will be a concern with \ngusts up to 35-40 mph. A Wind Advisory is in effect across S TX \nstarting this morning. Winds are expected to decrease below advisory \nlevels initially across the western areas this afternoon, then \nacross the eastern areas tonight. The islands are expected to remain \nat advisory levels through Saturday morning. \n\nGale conditions are expected later this morning as the cold front \npushes offshore, and will likely continue through Saturday morning \nto early Saturday afternoon. Seas are forecast to build to 8-13 \nfeet. Winds begin to relax Saturday afternoon and evening, but will \nremain at advisory levels through Saturday evening. \n\nAnother concern will be coastal flooding. The strong northerly winds \nand large seas across the gulf waters and swell periods of 8-9 \nseconds will likely lead to minor coastal flooding during times of \nhigh tide. A Coastal Flood Advisory is in effect this afternoon \nthrough Sunday morning. In addition, the long period swells will be \nconducive for a moderate rip current risk today through Saturday. \nThe north winds along the shore tend to negate the rip current risk, \nbut given the long period swells and large seas, should lead to a \nmoderate risk.",
